<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emerald Mound</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1250</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emerald Mound is a large Mississippian-period platform mound near Natchez, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, built from about 1250 by ancestors of the Natchez people. Covering about 3 hectares, it is one of the largest such mounds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, an earthen platform topped by smaller mounds for temples or elite dwellings. It was largely abandoned by the late 17th century, when the Natchez centre had moved to the Grand Village.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emerald-mound.jpg" alt="Emerald Mound" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elisa Rolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emerald_Mound_Site.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
